  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1220PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nH5 ridge begins to flatten as series of waves move north of the area \nthrough the period.  The first wave is expected to drag a surface \nboundary across the area Tuesday into Wednesday and bring with it \nchances for precipitation.  Later in the period, zonal flow will \nhelp to warm temperatures across the area.  Guidance begins to \ndiffer in overall flow late in the period, however there are \nindications of a deep trof forming across the western CONUS.  This \ntrof would lead to an increase in low-level moisture for area. We \nwill need to watch how this evolves for the rest of the week.\n\nLooking to Tuesday into Wednesday, there is a level 1 of 5 severe \nweather risk for mainly northern parts of the area as a wave drags a \nfront into our area.  Deep layer shear of 40 to 50kts is orientated \nparallel to this boundary suggested more multicell storms.  With \nlimited SBCAPE and 0-3km CAPE, any severe weather would likely be \nlarge hail.  What appears to be the main impact is heavy rain. With \nE/W alignment of the front along with a H85 40 to 50 kt LLJ expect \nplentiful moisture for heavy rain and the shear to keep organizing \nupdrafts leading storm longevity. Since crops are starting to dry, \nwe may see more water issues than anything.\n\nPast Wednesday, flow aloft transitions to more zonal, returning us \nto more seasonable temperatures for yet another brief reprieve. \nHowever, increasing heat and humidity appear possible towards the \nend of the week, continuing above normal temperatures through mid-\nSeptember.&&",
